==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} ''[[ditie]]'' or ''[[dité]]'', from ''[[ditier]]'', from {{etyl|la|en}} ''[[dictare|dictāre]]'' (participle ''[[dictatus]]'').

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈdɪti/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-ditty.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-uk-ditty.ogg|Audio (UK)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪti|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|ditties}}

# A short [[verse]] or [[tune]].
#: ''The Acme mattress '''ditty''' has been stuck in my head all day.''
#* Milton
#*: Religious, martial, or civil ditties.
#* Sandys
#*: And to the warbling lute soft '''ditties''' sing.
# A [[saying]] or [[utterance]], especially one that is short and frequently repeated.
#* Spenser
#*: O, too high '''ditty''' for my simple rhyme.

====Translations====
{{trans-top|a short verse or tune}}
* Chinese:
*: Mandarin: {{t+|cmn|小曲|tr=xiǎoqǔ|sc=Hani}}
{{trans-mid}}
* Maori: {{t|mi|ruriruri}}, {{t|mi|pao}}, {{t|mi|hakirara}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|пе́сенка|f}}, {{t+|ru|моти́в|m}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|ditties|dittying|dittied}}

# To [[sing]]; to [[warble]] a little [[tune]].
#* Herbert
#*: Beasts fain would sing; birds '''ditty''' to their notes.

